A||||2||Hawkman||Time for a valve job...||||5||
Z||000000||Hawkman||05-15-2000||01:47 PM||||Well it's about time to get the valve job done on my '85 2.5L engine.<P>Questions:<BR>1. What would the average cost of a valve job be if I took it to a mechanic?<P>2. Should I buy a remanufactured head and do it myself?  (novice mechanic)<P>3. If I do it myself what parts do I need?  (gaskets, head bolts, etc).<P>What potential "gotchas" should I expect?<P>What special tools are needed?<P><BR>Thanks for your help!||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||05-15-2000||10:26 PM||||ALWAYS replace the head bolts, especially on the 4 cyl. They came weak from the factory on the 4 cyl. They likely won't take reusing well. (There are several GM TSB's about this. It's also part of the fire recall papers.)<P>The rest is in haynes & chilton books. One of the few things they got right. Follow the tourqe rules they have. You'll want a torque wrench and a protractor head on a breaker bar. You torque the bolts as listed then rotate them the required number of degrees with the breaker bar and protractor.<P>The protractor can be a little hard to find. some autoparts places don't have them. sears might. It's an extension with a dial on it. there is a pointer you anchor to something solid. Set the pointer to zero then pull the breaker bar the required # of degrees. Check the number for each bolt, they are not all the same. (The bolt thread should be oiled before install.)<P>from TSB # 87-6(gas)-94 7/89<P>bolt # by position --&gt; GM part #<BR>1-8 --&gt; 10044566<BR>9 --&gt; 10044568<BR>10 --&gt; 10044570<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Hawkman||05-16-2000||07:28 AM||||Thanks for the help.<P>I will definitely order the new head bolts.<P>Harbor Freight Tools sells a "torque angle guage" for $7.95.  It's described "used to turn fasteners a specified number of degrees. Complete 360 degree angle scale marked in 2,10, and 30 degree increments. Indicator is etched in clear plate, protecting it from dirt and grease. Adjustable arm keeps angle scale stationary".  Will this do the job?<P>Check it out at harborfreight.com.<P>Thanks again for the advice!<BR>||||reg||1
